% ============================================================================
% sentence-type — an example sentence and which of the four grammatical
% sentence types it is (adj-facts-stdlib, LANGUAGE).
% ============================================================================
% English sentences come in four types, sorted by what they DO: a
% declarative sentence makes a statement, an interrogative sentence asks a
% question, an imperative sentence gives a command, and an exclamatory
% sentence shows strong feeling. Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? sentence_type(bears_dont_eat_when_they_hibernate, $Type)
%       % declarative
%
% A native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5): each `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `sentence_type(example, type)` carrying the source citation, so the lookup
% above is the ordinary SLD binding query — the engine returns the type WITH
% its source, on the CPU, with zero model calls, and abstains on any
% sentence the source does not classify this way. The query also runs
% BACKWARD — bind a type and recall which example sentence it names:
%
%     ? sentence_type($Example, imperative)
%       % sweep_the_floor_before_you_mop_it
%
% DESIGN NOTE — why `example` is a short atom-style label rather than the
% full sentence text: `fable-moral.adj` (shipped earlier in this loop)
% discovered that the ADJ query grammar accepts a quoted-string literal as
% a `table` row VALUE but NOT as a query ARGUMENT — a query can only ground
% an atom/number or bind a $Var. Using a short atom label here (the same
% discipline `vocabulary-in-context.adj` already established) keeps BOTH
% the direct query (`sentence_type(example, $Type)`) and the reverse query
% (`sentence_type($Example, type)`) usable as ordinary ground-argument
% binding queries, without hitting that limitation.
%
% Provenance: quoted verbatim from Grammarly's "4 Types of Sentences to
% Know, With Examples" article, which gives one worked example sentence per
% type:
%
%     example (this table's atom label)             type            example sentence (verbatim)
%     ----------------------------------------------  --------------  ---------------------------------------------
%     bears_dont_eat_when_they_hibernate               declarative     "Bears don't eat when they hibernate."
%     what_is_your_next_class                          interrogative   "What is your next class?"
%     sweep_the_floor_before_you_mop_it                 imperative      "Sweep the floor before you mop it."
%     myra_had_the_baby                                 exclamatory     "Myra had the baby!"
%
% WebFetch-verified before writing. `trust consensus` — Grammarly is a
% reputable, widely-cited grammar-content company, the same tier this
% stdlib already reserves for other non-.gov language sources (7ESL,
% Speakspeak). A sentence that is NOT one of these four — e.g. "the cat sat
% on the mat," a real, well-formed declarative sentence, but not one this
% specific cited page names — is deliberately NOT a row, so a recall
% ABSTAINS on it rather than inventing a classification the source never
% gave. (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table sentence_type {
    columns example, type

    row (bears_dont_eat_when_they_hibernate, declarative)
    row (what_is_your_next_class, interrogative)
    row (sweep_the_floor_before_you_mop_it, imperative)
    row (myra_had_the_baby, exclamatory)

    source "Bears don't eat when they hibernate."
    locator "https://www.grammarly.com/blog/sentences/kinds-of-sentences/"
    trust consensus
}
